{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>For He will cut off, etc. the nations, etc. Take heed to yourself, etc.</b> One must know: First, why did He attach the warning against idolatry to ״for He will cut off״ — which would imply that without this, there is no warning when he sees the nations serving idolatry peacefully and securely for many years; but this is not so, for the warning against idolatry does not change depending on what happens to the nations. Second, His saying <b>lest you be ensnared, etc. after they are destroyed</b> — but on the contrary, after he sees that they were destroyed before him, he would not be ensnared after them, and the words of the luminary Rashi, of blessed memory, did not suffice to settle the verse properly. Third, His saying <b>lest you be ensnared and lest you inquire</b> — since the ensnaring is the inquiring after their worship, He should have said ״lest you be ensnared, etc. to inquire after,״ etc. Fourth, His saying <b>saying</b> — to whom do they say? Fifth, His saying <b>How</b> — the intent of this statement is not known: whether it is a question, or an exclamation, or a comparison, and for all interpretations the precise meaning is unknown. Sixth, His saying <b>You shall not add to it and you shall not diminish</b> — it is difficult: well, ״you shall not diminish״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, commands that one not diminish from all the warnings He commanded concerning idolatry; but ״you shall not add״ — what does it come to teach? If it is not to add safeguards, this is not so, for it is written (Leviticus 18:30) ״וּשְׁמַרְתֶּם אֶת מִשְׁמַרְתִּי,״ and we find that our Sages, of blessed memory, were stringent to make a fence around everything resembling idolatry; and if it is to prohibit types of idolatry that the verse did not mention — behold, we find that the verse prohibited every type of idolatry." "<b>Indeed,</b> the intent of the verse is that it comes to warn against the errors into which a person may stray from the spectacle that his eyes behold — that the wicked are cut off from the land — and he will say, whichever way you look at it, one cannot avoid saying one of two things: either you will say that this worship that the cut-off nations practice is considered significant worship, and therefore HaShem was particular about their doing it for another and not for Him and blotted them out from the land of the living; or you will say that this worship that the nations practice is not considered worship worthy of arousing the jealousy of El, a zealous and avenging El — as you would say of one who throws to Mercurius, who merely makes a gesture of voiding, or one who passes a child to Molech, and similar things — and the proof is that this worship is not performed for HaShem Elohim, and the reason HaShem was zealous to cut off the nations was not because they practiced such worship, but because they did not serve HaShem with fitting worship and abandoned the rest of the commandments." "And whichever of these two alternatives a person decides upon in his mind, this constitutes nullification of the Torah. If he decides to say that the worship is fitting worship, logic would bring him to serve HaShem our Elohim with those very types of worship themselves, with which He was zealous when they were performed for another. And if he decides to say that it is not worship, logic would bring him to do likewise, since this is not an act of idolatry. Therefore the word of HaShem came and said <b>for He will cut off, etc. Take heed to yourself</b> — lest you err in one of the two alternatives recorded. Corresponding to the error arising from their destruction — that their worship is [genuine] worship — He said ״take heed to yourself״ <b>lest you be ensnared after them</b>, meaning ״you be ensnared״ as Rashi explained: you will be drawn after them to become attached to perform like their deeds for the god whom I serve, since I see that HaShem was particular about their serving in this manner for another than Him — and this is His saying <b>after they are destroyed from before you.</b> And corresponding to the error arising from examining the worship — that it is degrading and this worship is not called worship, and HaShem would not be particular about it to perform it in voiding and the like — He said <b>and lest you inquire after their gods</b>, etc., meaning, you will inquire into the manner of the worship, and from your own intellectual engagement with it you will say — and this is His saying <b>saying, How do these nations serve their gods</b>, meaning, it is an astonishing thing, for how could they perform such degrading worship? This is not considered worship, and I can do likewise, for there is here neither honor nor affection for idolatry — and this is His saying <b>and I will do so as well</b> — to void and to throw a stone at the cairn of Mercurius: take heed lest you say so." "<b>And He concluded</b> by saying <b>You shall not do so to HaShem</b> — this corresponds to the first error, that one might think to perform for HaShem a type of worship that the nations practice. And He uprooted the core reasoning from which the error stems, and said <b>for every abomination of HaShem that He hates they have done</b>, etc. — not as you think, that HaShem is zealous because they perform a praiseworthy deed for another than Him; it is not so, but rather it is a thing abhorrent in the eyes of HaShem. And He also adduced to say something foreign even in human eyes, as His saying <b>even their sons, etc. they burn</b> — and from now on also the second error, that this worship is not worship and HaShem is not particular about these deeds, is refuted by this in the pleasantness of His words, for the deed in and of itself HaShem hates, even though it involves no elevation or honor — and this is His saying <b>the abomination of HaShem that He hates they have done for their gods</b>, meaning, the deed in and of itself is abhorrent in the eyes of HaShem, in the way that many things are abhorrent without a perceptible reason to every discerning person — as you would say the prohibition of two sisters even though both desire the matter, and similar things; likewise He prohibited this deed for idolatry even though there is no desire that it be performed for His holy worship." "<b>And He concluded</b> by saying <b>the entire matter, etc. you shall not add, etc. and you shall not diminish.</b> According to our words it will be explained correctly: meaning, <b>you shall not add</b> — corresponding to the worship of the nations for their gods: one shall not make from them for the worship of HaShem. And <b>you shall not diminish from it</b> — meaning, to permit the degrading acts of idolatrous worship as mentioned; rather, everything is included in the prohibition. And with this all the fine points are resolved."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:17:18.871095009Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}